[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'July 1964, Silverstone test track. Rootes engineer Peter Harper feels the front tires break loose under torque nobody expected from a Sunbeam Alpine\'s engine bay. Ford\'s 260 cubic inch V8 sits where a four-cylinder was meant to'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fit. Nobody at Rootes built a hybrid by accident. They built a direct rival to the AC Cobra hidden inside a body designed for gentle Sunday drives. Newspapers called it Britain\'s fastest'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'surprise. Three years later Chrysler killed it. Not for lacking speed, but for the wrong name stamped on its heart. Comment tiger if you already know why it really died. Rootes Group built its'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reputation on comfort, not conquest. Through the 1950s, the Coventry-based company sold family saloons and mild-mannered roadsters to buyers who wanted reliability over drama. Nothing'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in that lineup hinted at a muscle car waiting to happen. Everything changed in 1962 when Carroll Shelby dropped a Ford V8 into an AC Ace and created the Cobra. Overnight, small British roadsters that'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'once competed on handling alone found themselves outgunned on straightaways, too. Rootes executives watched sales figures for their Sunbeam Alpine start sliding against this new competition and they needed an answer fast. Ian Garrad'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ran Rootes\' West Coast operations out of Los Angeles, close enough to the sports car scene to feel the shift happening in real time. Californian buyers wanted power and the Alpine simply couldn\'t'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'deliver it. Garrad reached out to Carroll Shelby directly asking whether a V8 conversion was even possible inside the Alpine\'s modest chassis. Shelby\'s team said yes, cautiously. Space under'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Alpine\'s bonnet was tight, tighter than anything Shelby had squeezed a V8 into before. Ken Miles, already famous for his work refining the Cobra, took the assignment personally, treating the'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conversion less like a side project and more like a genuine engineering puzzle worth solving properly. Two prototypes emerged within months, built independently by different teams racing'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to prove the concept. Shelby\'s California shop produced one version. A separate effort inside Rootes\' own Jensen Motors facility in England produced another, unaware the American team had already gotten there first.'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Both versions worked. Neither team knew the other existed until results landed on the same desk in Coventry. Rootes chose the Jensen-built version for production, partly for cost reasons and'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'partly because English assembly meant tighter control over quality. That decision mattered less than what both prototypes had already proven. A Ford engine could fit inside a British body without destroying the car\'s balance'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'entirely, provided engineers reworked the steering rack, the suspension mounts, and the entire front subframe to handle power the Alpine was never designed to carry. Test drivers noticed the difference immediately. Where the'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'standard Alpine felt composed and predictable, this new version felt like something barely contained. Acceleration figures nearly doubled. Top speed climbed well past 100 miles an hour,'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'territory the four-cylinder Alpine had never approached. Engineers who drove early prototypes described a car that rewarded confidence and punished hesitation in equal measure. Naming it took surprisingly'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little debate. Rootes wanted something that captured raw aggression without straying too far from the Alpine\'s existing identity. Tiger fit both requirements, aggressive enough to signal the transformation under the'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hood, familiar enough to reassure existing Sunbeam customers this was still recognizably their brand. Launch came in April 1964 at the New York International Auto Show, timed deliberately to catch American buyers'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'already hungry for exactly this kind of performance. Reviewers who tested it praised the sheer thrill of that V8 pulling hard through gears clearly designed for a much smaller engine. Some noted the handling required real attention at'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'speed, a fair trade for the power now sitting under the bonnet. Peter Harper\'s Silverstone session that July confirmed what Shelby\'s team already suspected back in California. The Tiger genuinely could compete with cars costing far'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more, provided the driver respected exactly how much torque now ran through a chassis built for something gentler. Front tires struggled to put that power down cleanly on corner exit, and Harper adjusted his inputs accordingly,'

[04:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'learning the car\'s real limits in real time rather than trusting numbers on a spec sheet. What nobody inside Rootes fully appreciated yet was how this engine, borrowed from a rival manufacturer thousands of miles away,'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'would eventually determine the Tiger\'s entire fate. For now, the car simply worked, faster than anything wearing a Sunbeam badge had ever gone before, and buyers on both sides of the Atlantic'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'were about to find that out for themselves. Buyers found out fast. Within its first year on sale, the Tiger built a reputation that outpaced even Rootes\' own marketing department. Road &amp; Track called it genuinely quick for its price.'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Car and Driver praised the raw character hiding inside what still looked, from a distance, like an ordinary British roadster. Racing success followed close behind. Rootes entered the Tiger into rally'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'events and production car races across Europe and America, and results started landing that surprised even Ken Miles\' own team. A Tiger finished third in its class at the 1964 Alpine Rally, an event named,'

[05:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coincidentally, after the very car this one had replaced under the hood. Class wins piled up at smaller American production races throughout 1965, each one reinforcing the idea that'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Rootes had built something genuinely capable, not just a marketing gimmick wrapped in a familiar shape. Celebrity ownership added momentum nobody could buy through advertising alone. Actor James Garner, already a serious amateur'

[05:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'racer, bought one and praised its balance publicly. Diana Rigg drove one in an episode of a popular British television series, cementing an image the Tiger hadn\'t consciously chased but'

[06:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'happily accepted. Suddenly this wasn\'t just a fast Sunbeam. It had become something closer to a symbol, proof that a modest British manufacturer could build a genuine muscle car and hold its own against'

[06:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'America\'s biggest names. If stories like this where an underdog engineering gamble actually pays off against long odds are the kind of thing that keeps you watching, subscribing costs nothing'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and helps more of these forgotten wins reach people who\'ve never heard them. Sales climbed steadily through 1965 and into 1966, concentrated heavily in the American'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'market where V8 power mattered most to buyers. Rootes expanded production to keep pace and for a company that had recently worried about losing ground entirely to the Cobra, the turnaround felt almost vindicating. Executives who'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'once viewed the V8 conversion as a defensive scramble now treated it as one of the smartest bets the company had ever made. Engineers kept refining the car throughout this stretch, addressing exactly the handling concerns Peter'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Harper had flagged during those early Silverstone sessions. A Mark II version arrived in 1967, fitted with a larger 289 cubic inch V8 and revised suspension geometry that tamed some of the original'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'car\'s rawness without dulling what made it exciting in the first place. On paper, everything pointed toward a car settling into a long successful production life. Underneath that success though, a much larger story was already'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reshaping the ground the Tiger stood on. Chrysler had spent years quietly acquiring shares in the Rootes Group, and by 1967, that stake had grown into outright control. American executives now ran a'

[07:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'British company that had just built its biggest recent win around a Ford engine, a detail that mattered far more to Chrysler than any sales figure or magazine review ever would. Nobody driving a Tiger off a dealership lot in'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'early 1967 had any reason to sense the collision heading toward the car they just bought. Production continued. Orders kept arriving. The Mark 2 sold well enough that Rootes had every'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'commercial reason to keep building it for years to come. Reason, though, was about to run into something it couldn\'t argue with. A company doesn\'t typically keep selling a car built around its biggest rival\'s engine once that rival\'s'

[08:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engine sits inside a factory the rival itself now controls. The Tiger\'s success had never depended on which company built its V8. Its survival, it turned out, depended entirely on that detail, and the answer'

[08:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to that question was about to arrive from Detroit rather than from any showroom. Detroit\'s answer arrived quietly through paperwork rather than any public announcement. Chrysler\'s takeover of Rootes completed in 1967,'

[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and almost immediately executives reviewing the company\'s model lineup landed on one uncomfortable fact. The Tiger, Rootes\' most celebrated recent success, ran entirely on an engine built'

[09:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by Ford. Selling a car powered by a direct competitor wasn\'t a minor inconsistency Chrysler could quietly overlook. It was a contradiction sitting at the center of the company\'s own showroom floor. A British sports car'

[09:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wearing a Rootes badge while running an engine assembled by the one manufacturer Chrysler existed to compete against. No amount of sales success could make that arrangement acceptable to a new parent company built around selling its own'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engines, not a rival\'s. Engineers inside Rootes explored alternatives before accepting defeat. Fitting a Chrysler V8 into the Tiger\'s engine bay seemed, on paper, like a straightforward substitution. Reality'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'proved far messier. The Tiger\'s compact chassis had been engineered specifically around the Ford engine\'s dimensions, down to mounting points, exhaust routing, and steering clearance worked out through years of careful refinement.'

[10:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Chrysler\'s own V8, physically larger and shaped differently, simply didn\'t fit without a redesign extensive enough to essentially create a new car. Cost made that redesign impossible to justify. A'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'car selling steadily but modestly, built by a subsidiary Chrysler had only just acquired, didn\'t warrant the kind of engineering investment a full chassis rework would demand. Executives faced a'

[10:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'choice between spending heavily to save a car built around a rival\'s technology, or simply letting production end on schedule and redirecting that money elsewhere. They chose the second option. Production of the Sunbeam Tiger stopped'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in June 1967, roughly 3 years after that first Silverstone test session confirmed the concept actually worked. Fewer than 7,000 Tigers had been built in total, a number that would have looked modest'

[10:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even without the abrupt ending, but one that now carried a strange weight given exactly why the line closed. Here\'s what makes this ending genuinely different from a typical corporate cancellation story. The Tiger didn\'t die because'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'customers stopped buying it, and it didn\'t die because a rival built something faster or cheaper. It died because the very engine that made it remarkable in the first place belonged, on paper, to the wrong family once that'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'paperwork changed hands. Chrysler didn\'t kill a failing product. It killed a successful one for carrying the wrong identity once ownership shifted underneath it. Peter Harper, still racing Tigers competitively'

[11:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through this period, watched the announcement land with the same disbelief many owners felt. A car proven on race tracks across two continents, praised by serious drivers and casual buyers alike, was ending not because'

[11:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything about its performance had changed, but because the badge above its steering wheel now answered to a different family than the engine below it. Root\'s engineers reportedly floated the idea of sourcing a suitable Chrysler'

[11:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engine that could work with minimal modification, hoping a partial fix might extend the car\'s life even briefly. Nothing came of those proposals. Chrysler\'s leadership had already decided the contradiction wasn\'t worth'

[12:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solving. A subsidiary running a rival\'s engine sent a message the new parent company had no interest in sending, regardless of what enthusiasts or dealers thought about the decision. What happened to the Tiger wasn\'t a failure'

[12:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of engineering, marketing, or demand. It was a car caught inside a corporate identity crisis it never chose for itself, built around a heart that made perfect sense under one owner, and became an open contradiction under the'

[12:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next. Few production cancellations in automotive history hinged this directly on a single component\'s parentage, rather than its performance, its cost, or its popularity.'

[12:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The Tiger had earned every bit of the reputation it built between 1964 and 1967. None of that reputation mattered once the company holding its keys decided the engine inside it belonged to the wrong'

[12:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'side of an old rivalry. Fewer than 7,000 Tigers ever left the factory, and even fewer survived today with their original Ford heart still intact. Some owners quietly swapped in different engines just to keep the badge'

[13:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'legal at home. Which surviving Tiger would you want to find? Tell me below, and subscribe for the next one.'
